WEBVTT

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.840
This beautiful knit weave stitch pattern

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.330
combines automatic patterning to form
the knit weave floats

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:14.260
along with picking up the long
floats manually for graphic appeal.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.460
The DesignaKnit stitch
pattern looks like this.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.300
The weave over symbol selects needles

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:24.460
and the weaving yarn
passes over those needles.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:29.460
However, DesignaKnit  does not have
a symbol for picking up those long floats.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.240
We will Follow this diagram
for picking up the floats.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.080
Looking at the punch card,

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:40.740
the black squares or the holes
indicate the weaving floats.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:44.680
The white squares are stitches that knit.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.760
Let's walk through the 16 row
repeat for this stitch pattern.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.220
I'm using a Brother machine,
so the needles are pre-selecting.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.940
But with Singer, Silver, Studio,
and other machines,

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:08.680
follow your manual for knit weave and pick
up the floats as shown in the diagram.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.560
The first pass of my carriage
sets up the first row.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.290
I like to manually lay the weaving yarn

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.400
over the selected needles, but you can
use the knit weave method of your choice.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.920
When I knit across my first row,
notice there are seven stitches between

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:33.480
the selected needles and There's
a long float between those needles.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:39.800
The second row of the pattern is the same,
creating another seven-stitch float.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.640
Now on my Brother machine,

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.660
I can see that there are no
needles selected for the next row.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.780
It will knit plain.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.140
Keeping the weaving yarn out of the way,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.960
according to the pattern,
I'll just knit two rows.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.460
The pattern has preselected
groups of three needles.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:05.380
Before I weave the next row, however,
I'll pick up both of those large floats

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:10.480
from below and hang them
on the center of a group of three.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.200
I'll do this across the row.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:25.680
Then, according to the pattern,
I'll weave the next four rows.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.380
Notice the shorter floats.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:36.060
The next sequence of two
rows creates long floats.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.890
Notice that the long floats are

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.080
alternating in position
from the first group of long floats.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.440
When these two rows are complete,
I'll knit two plain rows.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.300
Groups of three needles are selected.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:58.880
Before knitting the next section,
pick up the long floats and hang them

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:03.780
on the center needle of the groups
of three, just like before.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.080
Knit four rows of short floats again.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:20.200
And repeat the sequence starting
with two rows of long floats.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.900
Here's my booboo.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.760
As I was working this stitch pattern,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:28.340
I caught myself passing my carriage too
far from the edge of the knitting,

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:35.460
forming loops on edges. This is something
to watch for any time you're knitting.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:40.340
In this case, I was more worried about
positioning that weaving yarn and lost

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.880
focus about how far I
was passing my carriage.

